Right—with one naming clarification:

ACT means Acceptance and Commitment Therapy. Acceptance is one mechanism inside ACT, while the complete ACT model is a larger flexibility loop.

The acceptance sub-loop looks like:

Unwanted internal experience appears
        ↓
Notice the impulse to control, suppress, or escape it
        ↓
Distinguish external danger from internal discomfort
        ↓
Open attention toward the experience
        ↓
Describe sensations without judgment or unnecessary story
        ↓
Allow the experience to exist without obeying it
        ↓
Recover behavioral choice
        ↓
Take a value-aligned action
        ↓
Experience returns or changes
        ↺

Formally:

\text{Acceptance}
\neq
\text{approval}
\neq
\text{resignation}
\neq
\text{inaction}

Instead:

\text{Acceptance}
=
\text{discontinue futile internal control}
+
\text{preserve behavioral agency}

The control loop often works like this:

Pain
  → resistance
  → monitoring
  → amplification
  → more resistance
  ↺

Acceptance interrupts it:

Pain
  → willing contact
  → reduced struggle
  → freed attention
  → chosen action

In programming terms:

FUNCTION AcceptanceLoop(experience):
    IF experience signals actionable_external_danger:
        return ProtectiveAction
    control_strategy = detect_internal_control_strategy(experience)
    costs = evaluate(
        short_term_relief(control_strategy),
        long_term_restriction(control_strategy)
    )
    IF user_is_willing:
        observed = contact_experience(
            sensation_only = true,
            judgment_optional = true,
            suppression_required = false
        )
        choice_space = recover_available_actions(observed)
        action = choose_by_values(choice_space)
        return action, observed_delta
    ELSE:
        return PreserveConsentAndReduceStepSize

So the architecture contains nested cycles:

ACT psychological-flexibility cycle
    ├── Acceptance loop
    ├── Defusion loop
    ├── Present-moment attention loop
    ├── Self-as-context loop
    ├── Values clarification loop
    └── Committed-action loop

They can invoke one another. Acceptance makes room for discomfort; defusion removes a thought’s command authority; values supply direction; committed action converts that direction into behavior.

A compact ACT runtime would therefore be:

\boxed{
\text{Notice}
\rightarrow
\text{Open}
\rightarrow
\text{Unhook}
\rightarrow
\text{Orient}
\rightarrow
\text{Act}
\rightarrow
\text{Learn}
\rightarrow
\text{Notice again}
}

The loop is completed not when the feeling disappears, but when the person regains enough freedom to choose what happens next.